Subject: [overlay 0040] the dlight stamp must name ONE pass — the per-eye lighting split

R14c measured the flicker in pixels for the first time: with rerelease data on base1 in VR,
the two eyes of ONE published pair disagree about LIGHTING on 100 % of stationary frames
(mean-luma delta 2.69 against a 0.10 threshold; the signed difference image shows a whole
wall filled solid). Left eye brighter in 69 of 70. Cleared by cl_shadowlights 0 / gl_dynamic 0
/ gl_per_pixel_lighting 0; q2vripd 0 gives byte-identical eyes.

R15 instrumented it (the instrument is kept below, `gl_lightdiag 1`) and the numbers name the
mechanism exactly. On base1, per host frame:

  LIGHTALIAS eye=0 nextdf=1490 prestamped=33      <-- must be 0
  LIGHTDIAG  eye=0 n=19 lh=783e21f5 marked=12860 bh=f7db4e4e
  LIGHTALIAS eye=1 nextdf=1490 prestamped=12837
  LIGHTDIAG  eye=1 n=19 lh=783e21f5 marked=12837 bh=3b11653c

NOT the 64-light cap. `lh`, an order-independent hash of the admitted dlight list, is
IDENTICAL between the eyes, and n=19 — the cap is not even reached. It could not have been
per-eye: CL_AddEntities calls CL_CalcViewValues first, which resets cl.refdef.vieworg to the
GAME origin, and V_AddLightEx's distance fade reads that — the eye offset is applied later in
V_RenderView. Overlay 0039's priority eviction is untouched and still correct.

It is the STAMP. glr.dlightframe is supposed to name one render pass, and tess.c's gate
(0039) trusts it. But the counter advances by only +1 per HOST frame — 0031 rewinds the
repeat render to dlightframe_base, and 0039 restores the world's stamp after each inline
bmodel — while every pass CONSUMES two distinct values: base+1 for the world, base+2 for
every bmodel. So frame N's bmodel stamp IS frame N+1's world stamp. Faces the previous
frame's bmodel pass stamped therefore read as freshly marked by this frame's world pass; they
belong to a submodel's nodes, so the world node walk never re-visits them to clear the bits,
and they carry the previous pass's dlightbits straight through the gate. `prestamped=33` is
those faces, and eye 0's marked count is exactly eye 1's plus that leak (12860 = 12837 + 23).

That asymmetry is the whole artefact: the FIRST eye sees the leaked faces lit, then its own
bmodel pass re-stamps them out of range, so the SECOND eye sees them unlit. A set of surfaces
lit in one eye only is binocular rivalry — it reads as a flash, and as the head starts and
stops the admitted set changes, which is the seven-round "walls switching light/dark" report.

Fix: track the high-water mark of the counter and start each non-repeat render from it, so a
new stamp is strictly greater than every stamp any face carries. Three lines plus the
bookkeeping. 0031's rewind still works (the repeat render reuses base+1/base+2 exactly, so
GL_MarkLights re-marks idempotently); 0039's gate now means what it says.

Upstream defect, upstreamable as-is and nothing stereo about it: a flat desktop carries the
same one-frame-late bmodel bits into its world pass, it just has no second eye to disagree
with. The 2D iOS build takes the identical path (repeat_frame is never set there).

Also kept: `gl_lightdiag` (default 0), the cvar-gated instrument above — LIGHTALIAS asserts
the prestamped==0 invariant, LIGHTDIAG separates a per-eye light SELECTION difference from a
per-eye MARKING difference. It is the only way to see this class of defect without a pixel
harness.

